%I A399784 #21 Sep 21 2026 17:30:22
%S A399784 3,6,32,34,472,1593,10581,34735,110623,302047,387967,742071,737109,
%T A399784 978570,2169833,1127178,2972046,5769719,12591941,11503279
%N A399784 Power loops: the number of circular permutations (reversals not counted as different) of the numbers 1 to n such that the sum of any two consecutive numbers is a perfect power.
%C A399784 There are no solutions for n <= 16.
%C A399784 The first and last numbers of these permutations are required to have a perfect power sum when the numbers are written in a row (see Example section).
%C A399784 Note that "circular" and "loop" describe the same type of permutation.
%C A399784 _Giovanni Resta_ found that the smallest n for which a cubic loop exists is 473 (see Carlos Rivera link), while for squares it is n = 32 (A071984), and for perfect powers it is n = 17.
%C A399784 This sequence, together with A399783, A399785 and A399786, gives the four possible distinct counts of power chains.
%C A399784 In terms of graph theory, these numbers count Hamiltonian cycles, with reversals not counted as distinct (see Diophante link).
%H A399784 Diophante, <a href="http://www.diophante.fr/problemes-par-themes/h-graphes-et-circuits/681-h103-les-chaines-de-puissances">H103. Les chaînes de puissances</a> (in French).
%H A399784 Carlos Rivera, <a href="https://www.primepuzzles.net/puzzles/puzz_311.htm">Puzzle 311: Sum to a cube</a>, The Prime Puzzles & Problems Connection.
%F A399784 a(n) = A399785(n) - A399783(n).
%F A399784 a(n) = (A399786(n) - A399783(n)) / n.
%F A399784 a(n) = (A399786(n) - A399785(n)) / (n-1) for n > 1.
%e A399784 a(17) = 3, since there are three power loops of minimum length = 17:
%e A399784   [1-8-17-10-6-2-7-9-16-11-14-13-3-5-4-12-15] as 15 + 1 = 2^4,
%e A399784   [1-8-17-10-6-3-13-14-2-7-9-16-11-5-4-12-15] as 15 + 1 = 2^4,
%e A399784   [1-8-17-15-10-6-2-7-9-16-11-14-13-12-4-5-3] as 3 + 1 = 2^2.
%Y A399784 The four possible distinct counts of power chains: A399783 (number of linear permutations), this sequence (number of circular permutations), A399785 (number of total permutations counting each circular solution only once), A399786 (number of total permutations counting each circular solution n times).
%Y A399784 Cf. A001597 (perfect powers), A071984 (similar, but with square loops).
%K A399784 nonn,more,new
%O A399784 17,1
%A A399784 _Bernard Schott_, Sep 12 2026
%E A399784 a(24)-a(30) from _Sean A. Irvine_, Sep 20 2026
%E A399784 a(31)-a(36) from _Sean A. Irvine_, Sep 21 2026
